\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The audited setting and the three gated questions. \textbf  {(a)}~A teacher prefills a long context $x$ and stores its cache $C_T(x)$; a translator maps that stored cache into the student's cache space, and the student answers a short query $q$ from the translated cache $\hat  {C}_S(x)$. The red crossed path is the strict zero-re-prefill constraint: no context token enters the student's forward pass. The audit scores the handoff by the change in gold-letter probability against the student's own prefill (baseline), with the teacher's full prefill as the capability upper bound. \textbf  {(b)}~The three questions can fail independently (Section~\ref {sec:intro}), so each carries its own instrument and gate; $\dagger $ marks gates fixed before the runs, and $\ddagger $ marks families and axes added while revising and evaluated under the same gates (Section~\ref {sec:tiers}).}}{17}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:framework}{{1}{17}{The audited setting and the three gated questions. \textbf {(a)}~A teacher prefills a long context $x$ and stores its cache $C_T(x)$; a translator maps that stored cache into the student's cache space, and the student answers a short query $q$ from the translated cache $\hat {C}_S(x)$. The red crossed path is the strict zero-re-prefill constraint: no context token enters the student's forward pass. The audit scores the handoff by the change in gold-letter probability against the student's own prefill (baseline), with the teacher's full prefill as the capability upper bound. \textbf {(b)}~The three questions can fail independently (Section~\ref {sec:intro}), so each carries its own instrument and gate; $\dagger $ marks gates fixed before the runs, and $\ddagger $ marks families and axes added while revising and evaluated under the same gates (Section~\ref {sec:tiers})}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The mapper landscape for Qwen3-4B$\to $1.7B under the unified audit protocol: gold-probability CHG over the student's own prefill, with 95\% bootstrap CIs. All primary-pair point estimates are negative and no configuration passes the replacement gate; calibration budget moves affine mapping by only $0.005$ on a fixed evaluation set; the architecture-anchored RAT, the gradient-trained MLP families (hatched bars span independent trainings), and the over-parameterized joint MLP sit inside the same band. The RAT, MLP, and joint-MLP bars are frozen-gate extensions; the remaining bars are confirmatory (Section~\ref {sec:tiers}).}}{18}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:landscape}{{2}{18}{The mapper landscape for Qwen3-4B$\to $1.7B under the unified audit protocol: gold-probability CHG over the student's own prefill, with 95\% bootstrap CIs. All primary-pair point estimates are negative and no configuration passes the replacement gate; calibration budget moves affine mapping by only $0.005$ on a fixed evaluation set; the architecture-anchored RAT, the gradient-trained MLP families (hatched bars span independent trainings), and the over-parameterized joint MLP sit inside the same band. The RAT, MLP, and joint-MLP bars are frozen-gate extensions; the remaining bars are confirmatory (Section~\ref {sec:tiers})}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Oracle probes on Qwen3-4B$\to $1.7B ($n{=}30$; the fraction probe is confirmatory, the best-source replication and the octant sweep are frozen-gate extensions, Section~\ref {sec:tiers}). \textbf  {Left}: gold-letter probability as translated teacher content replaces the student's own cache, for two content sources. Filled markers mark mixture fractions whose paired CHG interval excludes zero: the decline separates from the student from $\alpha {=}0.5$ onward for the RAT source and only at $\alpha {=}0.75$ for the affine source, and no mixture at either source yields a gain whose interval excludes zero. \textbf  {Right}: window injection per student-layer octant (affine source), plotted as gold-probability CHG with $95\%$ CIs. Only the octant spanning layers $10$--$13$ excludes zero; two top-layer octants carry small positive point estimates whose intervals span zero.}}{18}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:probes}{{3}{18}{Oracle probes on Qwen3-4B$\to $1.7B ($n{=}30$; the fraction probe is confirmatory, the best-source replication and the octant sweep are frozen-gate extensions, Section~\ref {sec:tiers}). \textbf {Left}: gold-letter probability as translated teacher content replaces the student's own cache, for two content sources. Filled markers mark mixture fractions whose paired CHG interval excludes zero: the decline separates from the student from $\alpha {=}0.5$ onward for the RAT source and only at $\alpha {=}0.75$ for the affine source, and no mixture at either source yields a gain whose interval excludes zero. \textbf {Right}: window injection per student-layer octant (affine source), plotted as gold-probability CHG with $95\%$ CIs. Only the octant spanning layers $10$--$13$ excludes zero; two top-layer octants carry small positive point estimates whose intervals span zero}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Perplexity over the scoring suffix versus accuracy for every method and run in the audit protocol (log scale; the superseded v1.1 runs are excluded). All points use the suffix-scoring protocol, so the identity control is the student's suffix-native reference; the student's full-context native loss (25.4, Table~\ref {tab:main}) is a different quantity and is not plotted. The identity control sits at low perplexity and high accuracy, while translated caches, probe mixtures, and native content spread across the plane---including a point with near-native perplexity ($23.7$) whose accuracy stays $23$ points below the student's $0.500$. Perplexity does not order methods the way the task metric does.}}{19}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:decoupling}{{4}{19}{Perplexity over the scoring suffix versus accuracy for every method and run in the audit protocol (log scale; the superseded v1.1 runs are excluded). All points use the suffix-scoring protocol, so the identity control is the student's suffix-native reference; the student's full-context native loss (25.4, Table~\ref {tab:main}) is a different quantity and is not plotted. The identity control sits at low perplexity and high accuracy, while translated caches, probe mixtures, and native content spread across the plane---including a point with near-native perplexity ($23.7$) whose accuracy stays $23$ points below the student's $0.500$. Perplexity does not order methods the way the task metric does}{figure.4}{}}
